[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Module Resolution in TypeScript: Node vs. Bundler and CommonJS vs. ESModules"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Module resolution is the process the TypeScript compiler uses to figure out what a reference points to when you import a file. Understanding how TypeScript maps modules to files is critical for building robust applications, especially when navigating the differences between Node.js runtimes and modern bundlers like Webpack, Vite, or esbuild."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Four Pillars of TypeScript Module Resolution"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript provides several strategies for module resolution via the moduleResolution compiler option in tsconfig.json. The most common ones are node (often referred to as Node10), nodenext, and bundler."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"node: Mimics traditional Node.js CommonJS resolution behavior prior to Node 12."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"nodenext / node16: Implements modern Node.js ECMAScript Module (ESM) and CommonJS resolution rules, strictly enforcing package.json exports fields and file extensions."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"bundler: Designed for modern web bundlers that allow importing non-JS assets, extensionless imports, and relaxed package.json rules."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"CommonJS vs. ESModules (CJS vs. ESM)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Historically, Node.js used CommonJS (require and module.exports), which is synchronous and dynamic. Modern JavaScript uses ECMAScript Modules (import and export), which are statically analyzable and asynchronous."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// CommonJS Style (Legacy Node)
const fs = require('fs');
module.exports = { myFunction };

// ESModules Style (Modern standard)
import fs from 'node:fs';
export function myFunction() {}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Node.js Resolution Strategy"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using node or nodenext, TypeScript relies heavily on the environment configuration. Under nodenext, your TypeScript files must use explicit relative extensions in imports if they resolve to local files."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Valid under nodenext/ESM:
import { helper } from './helper.js';

// Invalid under nodenext (missing extension):
import { helper } from './helper';"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Bundler Resolution Strategy"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern bundlers do not run directly in Node.js during production; instead, they process files for the browser. The bundler resolution mode bridges the gap between Node-style package resolution and browser-style extensionless imports."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "target": "es2022"
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows extensionless relative imports (e.g., importing ./App instead of ./App.tsx)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows importing non-code assets like CSS, SVG, or JSON natively without throwing TypeScript compilation errors."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose 'nodenext' if you are writing a backend application intended to run natively on modern Node.js. Choose 'bundler' if your code is destined for Webpack, Vite, or a similar frontend bundling pipeline. Always match your 'module' setting with the appropriate 'moduleResolution' strategy to avoid runtime import failures."}]
